Scheduled jobs
gettingstarted-04-scheduled-jobs
Each row is a job: an SD command of yours that runs unattended, as your Windows user, whether or not you are signed in. The job clock, one Task Scheduler task, runs every minute and starts each job whose time has come. The columns are its name, its schedule, its command, when it runs next and when it last ran; how the command ended is in the audit trail. The box's title says when jobs are off.
N makes a job and C (or Enter) changes the selected one: a form asks for the name, the schedule (every 5, 10, 15, 20 or 30 minutes, hourly, daily hh:mm, weekly mon..sun hh:mm, monthly 1..28 hh:mm), the command, and your account password, which a job always needs. D deletes the job under the cursor after you type its name; R starts it now, through the job clock. Each is shown as its set.job command in a box answered Run or Cancel; Cancel is what Enter chooses.
Jobs are off as delivered. The administrator turns them on with set.jobs.access on (or on the gates page); while they are off nothing can be made or run, and every job is refused at its next run. A run missed while the computer is off, or more than five minutes late, is not made up, and jobs run only while SD runs, which its startup task starts at boot.
